## The development of supply and sales on the Bratislava new-build market

Supply grew and demand rose against the previous period. {qSalesNow} flats were sold against {qSalesThen} in the preceding {qDays} days. Sales were not distorted by an exceptionally high figure in one of the large projects — the strongest of them accounted for {qTopSellerN} flats. Taking that into account, demand genuinely rose against the previous period.

The average achieved price of sold flats rose slightly to {qSoldM2} €/m² incl. VAT, matching the average asking price. Behind the rise in the price of sold flats is above all increased demand in {qD1} and {qD2}, which are dearer than the average — in {qD1} sales rose from {qD1Then} to {qD1Now} flats and in {qD2} from {qD2Then} to {qD2Now}. The average absolute price of sold flats was {qSoldPrice} € incl. VAT at an average floor area of {qSoldAreaEn} m².

## A view on where the Bratislava new-build market is heading

Tomáš Kamhal, founder of Residata: "The Bratislava market is in a state where supply and sales are both rising and prices are standing still. Over the last two months {qNewUnits} flats were added to the offer and sales rose by {qSalesChangePctEn}% at the same time. So the market is absorbing the new supply for now.

The first factor is supply itself. There are {qSupply} flats for sale today across {qProjects} projects, and the number is rising. As long as the pace of sales keeps up, developers have no reason to cut prices — and the data bears that out: the average price moved by {qPanelM2ChangePctEn}% on a comparable sample of projects.

The second factor is demand, still constrained by interest rates and household purchasing power. The average flat sold cost {qSoldPrice} € and its average floor area rose to {qSoldAreaEn} m², so the buyers entering the market were going for larger flats.

Unless interest rates and household purchasing power change materially, we expect the current state to continue: supply will keep growing and prices will stay roughly where they are. What will be interesting to watch is the development of supply — further growth could in time start to push prices down."

**Author:** Tomáš Kamhal, founder of Residata · tomas@residata.eu
